<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry the Fowler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 876–<a href="/years/936">936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Henry I of Germany, Henry the Fowler of Saxony · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry the Fowler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> in 919, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>. He won over the dukes of Swabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, regained Lotharingia, campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from the Hevelli, and defeated the Hungarians at Riade in 933. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> became emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler.jpg" alt="Henry the Fowler" width="315" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Heinrich_I_Posse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Henry the Fowler</b>, as king, prepared for the Magyar threat by negotiating a nine-year truce in 926, building fortifications and training cavalry, and defeated the Magyars at the Battle of Riade in 933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Saxony, ruled by the Liudolfing family, produced the kings of East Francia from 919, beginning with <b>Henry the Fowler</b>, and his son Otto I was crowned emperor in 962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Louis the Child, the last Carolingian ruler of East Francia, died in 911, the dukes and magnates elected Conrad, Duke of Franconia, as their king, and in 919 chose the Saxon duke <b>Henry the Fowler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Henry the Fowler</b> of East Francia routed a Magyar army near the river Unstrut in northern Thuringia, after refusing to pay further tribute.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-15">15 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Fritzlar, the nobles of Franconia and Saxony elected <b>Henry the Fowler</b>, Duke of Saxony, king of East Francia, the first Saxon to take the throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles the Simple, king of West Francia, and <b>Henry the Fowler</b>, king of East Francia, signed a &quot;pact of friendship&quot; aboard a ship in the middle of the Rhine near Bonn.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Memleben, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Memleben, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.27,11.49&amp;year=915" aria-label="Memleben on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 388 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry the Fowler"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.27,11.49&amp;year=915">What was here in 915 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
